  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved gender-responsive social protection policies to extend coverage and enhance benefit adequacy for all, particularly herders and informal workers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mongolia&apos;s social protection system has evolved over a long period of time, achieving near-universal coverage throughout individuals&apos; life cycles via social insurance and social assistance programs. However, there are number of areas where social security coverage, particularly of informal workers and herders, and other key parameters affecting the level of benefits. Only around 20 per cent of herders and informal workers are covered by social insurance under a voluntary scheme.

The ILO has been actively engaged in social security reform in Mongolia since 1990s promoting C102 - Social Security (Minimum Standards) Convention and Social Protection Floors Recommendation, 2012 (No. 202), and facilitating national dialogue on social protection and evidence-based policy making, promoting employment and extending social protection to herders and informal workers. 

The Parliament of Mongolia has approved social insurance package law in July 2023 which went into effect from 1 January 2024 covering old-age, employment injury and occupational disease, and welfare pension. The law has made progress in some areas, for example, linking the benefits to inflation rate, equal age of retirement for men and women, and prohibition of retroactive contributions. However, there are other areas to improve the legislation, as well as enforcing the implementation of the new legislation.

The office provides support to the Government and social partners for effective implementation of the new Social Insurance Law towards increased social insurance coverage of herders and informal workers.

Following the approval of Labour Law in 2021 and Social Insurance package Law in 2023, there is a need to revise occupational safety and health law in Mongolia. The office assists the tripartite constituents in improving their capacity, facilitating dialogue and discussions, and providing knowledge sharing and technical comments.</narrative>
  </description>
